1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,102
At the start of the twentieth century,
every machine shop in the world had the

2
00:00:06,102 --> 00:00:06,952
same problem.

3
00:00:07,302 --> 00:00:09,137
Steel was getting harder.

4
00:00:09,487 --> 00:00:11,855
Machines were getting stronger.

5
00:00:12,205 --> 00:00:17,798
Electric motors and heavier lathes could
push a cutting tool faster than ever

6
00:00:17,798 --> 00:00:18,306
before.

7
00:00:18,656 --> 00:00:21,067
But the tool itself kept failing.

8
00:00:21,417 --> 00:00:27,065
Push it too fast and the edge began to
glow, then it softened, and within minutes

9
00:00:27,065 --> 00:00:27,902
it was gone.

10
00:00:28,252 --> 00:00:32,689
The machinist stopped the lathe, walked to
the grinder, and started again.

11
00:00:33,839 --> 00:00:38,831
Then an inventor in a small town in
Indiana made something strange.

12
00:00:39,181 --> 00:00:42,573
It was a metal with almost no iron in it
at all.

13
00:00:42,923 --> 00:00:44,353
It did not rust.

14
00:00:44,703 --> 00:00:46,260
It did not tarnish.

15
00:00:46,610 --> 00:00:52,167
And when it was heated to a dull red, the
temperature at which ordinary tool steel

16
00:00:52,167 --> 00:00:54,674
turns soft as butter, it stayed hard.

17
00:00:55,824 --> 00:00:58,149
He named it after the stars.

18
00:00:59,299 --> 00:01:05,006
For a few decades this alloy was one of
the most important cutting materials in

19
00:01:05,006 --> 00:01:05,657
industry.

20
00:01:06,007 --> 00:01:09,569
It helped feed the factories of the First
World War.

21
00:01:09,919 --> 00:01:13,226
It went into the engines that crossed the
Atlantic.

22
00:01:13,576 --> 00:01:19,080
And then, almost as quickly as it arrived,
it was pushed aside by something harder.

23
00:01:20,230 --> 00:01:26,331
Today almost nobody outside a machine shop
or a welding supply store has heard of it.

24
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:29,049
But it never really went away.

25
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:31,661
So what happened to Stellite?

26
00:01:33,611 --> 00:01:39,053
To understand why Stellite mattered, we
have to start with the oldest problem in

27
00:01:39,053 --> 00:01:39,733
machining.

28
00:01:40,883 --> 00:01:43,486
Cutting metal is a strange act.

29
00:01:43,836 --> 00:01:48,380
A lathe tool does not slice through steel
the way a knife cuts bread.

30
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:54,149
It pushes into the material and forces a
layer of it to shear away as a chip.

31
00:01:54,499 --> 00:01:59,000
That chip slides across the top of the
tool under enormous pressure.

32
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:04,543
The friction and the deformation turn
almost all of that work into heat, and

33
00:02:04,543 --> 00:02:09,121
most of that heat gathers in a tiny zone
right at the cutting edge.

34
00:02:10,271 --> 00:02:14,708
For most of the nineteenth century, tools
were made of carbon steel.

35
00:02:15,058 --> 00:02:20,878
Carbon steel can be made very hard by
heating it and quenching it in water or

36
00:02:20,878 --> 00:02:21,181
oil.

37
00:02:21,531 --> 00:02:23,813
But that hardness is borrowed.

38
00:02:24,163 --> 00:02:29,781
Heat the tool again, even to a few hundred
degrees, and the hardness starts to drain

39
00:02:29,781 --> 00:02:30,115
away.

40
00:02:30,465 --> 00:02:33,388
The steel tempers itself while it works.

41
00:02:33,738 --> 00:02:39,437
So the machinist had a simple rule: go
slow, keep it cool, and never let the edge

42
00:02:39,437 --> 00:02:40,351
change color.

43
00:02:41,501 --> 00:02:45,597
That rule set the speed of the entire
industrial world.

44
00:02:45,947 --> 00:02:51,331
It limited how fast a crankshaft could be
turned, how fast a gun barrel could be

45
00:02:51,331 --> 00:02:55,099
bored, and how many parts a factory could
make in a day.

46
00:02:57,049 --> 00:02:59,673
The first escape came from England.

47
00:03:00,023 --> 00:03:07,043
In 1868 a metallurgist named Robert
Forester Mushet developed a steel with

48
00:03:07,043 --> 00:03:09,794
tungsten and manganese in it.

49
00:03:10,144 --> 00:03:15,815
Mushet steel hardened simply by cooling in
air, and it held its edge longer than

50
00:03:15,815 --> 00:03:16,736
carbon steel.

51
00:03:17,086 --> 00:03:22,684
It was a real step forward, though it was
expensive and brittle, and most shops used

52
00:03:22,684 --> 00:03:24,617
it only for the hardest jobs.

53
00:03:25,767 --> 00:03:29,756
The bigger leap came at the end of the
century, in Pennsylvania.

54
00:03:30,106 --> 00:03:36,486
In 1899 and 1900, Frederick Winslow Taylor
and Maunsel White, working at the

55
00:03:36,486 --> 00:03:42,699
Bethlehem Steel Company, experimented with
heating tungsten tool steels to

56
00:03:42,699 --> 00:03:46,981
temperatures that everyone else considered
ruinous.

57
00:03:47,331 --> 00:03:52,387
The result was a steel that kept cutting
even when its edge ran hot.

58
00:03:52,737 --> 00:03:55,190
Cutting speeds roughly tripled.

59
00:03:56,340 --> 00:04:02,154
At the Paris Exposition of 1900, tools
made from the new steel were shown cutting

60
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:07,753
at speeds that seemed impossible, with the
edge running hot and still cutting.

61
00:04:08,103 --> 00:04:11,154
The demonstration caused a sensation.

62
00:04:11,504 --> 00:04:15,280
This was the birth of what we now call
high-speed steel.

63
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:20,185
High-speed steel changed machining almost
overnight.

64
00:04:20,535 --> 00:04:22,156
But it was still steel.

65
00:04:22,506 --> 00:04:27,733
It still depended on heat treatment for
its hardness, and it still had a limit.

66
00:04:28,083 --> 00:04:31,048
Push it hard enough and it, too, would
give up.

67
00:04:31,398 --> 00:04:34,257
The door was open for something different.

68
00:04:36,207 --> 00:04:40,175
The man who walked through that door was
not a steelmaker at all.

69
00:04:41,325 --> 00:04:47,917
Elwood Haynes was born in Portland,
Indiana, in 1857.

70
00:04:48,267 --> 00:04:55,085
He trained as a teacher and studied
chemistry, then worked in the natural gas

71
00:04:55,085 --> 00:04:59,424
industry that boomed across Indiana in the
1880s.

72
00:04:59,774 --> 00:05:05,309
He settled in the town of Kokomo, and
there he became famous for something else

73
00:05:05,309 --> 00:05:05,939
entirely.

74
00:05:06,289 --> 00:05:12,954
On July the fourth, 1894, Haynes test
drove one of the first gasoline

75
00:05:12,954 --> 00:05:16,721
automobiles built in the United States.

76
00:05:17,071 --> 00:05:19,162
He called it the Pioneer.

77
00:05:20,312 --> 00:05:23,000
But cars were only part of his story.

78
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:25,953
Haynes was obsessed with metals.

79
00:05:26,303 --> 00:05:33,064
Around 1895 he began searching for a new
hard alloy that would resist rusting,

80
00:05:33,064 --> 00:05:39,999
something he could use for engine parts
and, later, for cutlery and instruments.

81
00:05:40,349 --> 00:05:45,639
Iron was the obvious starting point for
almost every metallurgist of the time.

82
00:05:45,989 --> 00:05:48,656
Haynes went in another direction.

83
00:05:49,006 --> 00:05:54,610
He began experimenting with cobalt and
chromium, two metals that most of the

84
00:05:54,610 --> 00:05:56,601
steel industry barely used.

85
00:05:57,751 --> 00:06:00,055
What he found was remarkable.

86
00:06:00,405 --> 00:06:06,341
An alloy of cobalt and chromium, with no
iron to speak of, took a brilliant polish

87
00:06:06,341 --> 00:06:07,210
and kept it.

88
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:09,075
It did not stain.

89
00:06:09,425 --> 00:06:12,987
It did not tarnish in air or in many
acids.

90
00:06:13,337 --> 00:06:20,280
In 1907 Haynes received patents on two
binary alloys: one of nickel and chromium,

91
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,937
and one of cobalt and chromium.

92
00:06:24,087 --> 00:06:29,010
He chose a name from the Latin word
stella, meaning star, because of the

93
00:06:29,010 --> 00:06:32,087
bright, lasting shine of the metal's
surface.

94
00:06:32,437 --> 00:06:34,037
He called it Stellite.

95
00:06:35,987 --> 00:06:41,634
At first Haynes thought of Stellite as a
material for table knives, surgical and

96
00:06:41,634 --> 00:06:44,457
dental instruments and spark plug parts.

97
00:06:44,807 --> 00:06:46,556
But he kept experimenting.

98
00:06:46,906 --> 00:06:52,282
He added tungsten to the cobalt and
chromium, and the alloy changed character.

99
00:06:52,632 --> 00:06:56,216
It became much harder, hard enough to cut
other metals.

100
00:06:57,366 --> 00:07:04,596
In 1913 Haynes was granted a patent on
this three-part alloy of cobalt, chromium

101
00:07:04,596 --> 00:07:05,771
and tungsten.

102
00:07:06,121 --> 00:07:11,383
The patent described a range of
compositions, with tungsten anywhere from

103
00:07:11,383 --> 00:07:12,969
five to sixty percent.

104
00:07:13,319 --> 00:07:18,652
Haynes recommended a mix with roughly a
quarter to a half tungsten and around

105
00:07:18,652 --> 00:07:22,599
fifteen percent chromium, with cobalt
making up the rest.

106
00:07:23,749 --> 00:07:29,354
The courts later examined this alloy in a
patent dispute, and their description is

107
00:07:29,354 --> 00:07:30,448
worth repeating.

108
00:07:30,798 --> 00:07:36,246
They noted that ordinary tool steel held
its edge fairly well up to about six

109
00:07:36,246 --> 00:07:37,945
hundred degrees Celsius.

110
00:07:38,295 --> 00:07:44,040
Haynes's alloy kept its hardness from
around six hundred all the way up to about

111
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:45,548
one thousand degrees.

112
00:07:45,898 --> 00:07:51,123
The court called this red hardness
entirely novel among commercial alloys,

113
00:07:51,123 --> 00:07:56,419
and stated that in suitable work it
allowed double the cutting speed of the

114
00:07:56,419 --> 00:07:57,973
best high-speed steel.

115
00:07:59,123 --> 00:08:01,512
That was the secret of Stellite.

116
00:08:01,862 --> 00:08:03,889
It was not a steel at all.

117
00:08:04,239 --> 00:08:06,692
It was not hardened by quenching.

118
00:08:07,042 --> 00:08:09,709
It was hard as it came out of the mold.

119
00:08:10,059 --> 00:08:15,225
There was no temper to lose, so when the
edge glowed, nothing inside the metal

120
00:08:15,225 --> 00:08:15,755
changed.

121
00:08:16,105 --> 00:08:21,307
Stellite tools could be cast directly to
shape and ground to a cutting edge, and

122
00:08:21,307 --> 00:08:24,169
they kept on cutting where steel would
fail.

123
00:08:26,119 --> 00:08:32,117
Red hardness is worth a moment of its own,
because it is the whole story of cutting

124
00:08:32,117 --> 00:08:34,140
tool materials in two words.

125
00:08:35,290 --> 00:08:40,581
Every cutting tool material is a
compromise between hardness and toughness.

126
00:08:40,931 --> 00:08:43,896
Hardness lets the edge resist wear.

127
00:08:44,246 --> 00:08:47,510
Toughness lets it survive shocks without
chipping.

128
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:52,468
But at the cutting edge there is a third
enemy, and that is temperature.

129
00:08:52,818 --> 00:08:56,509
A tool can be hard on the bench and soft
in the cut.

130
00:08:57,659 --> 00:09:01,968
Carbon steel loses its hardness at a few
hundred degrees.

131
00:09:02,318 --> 00:09:05,454
High-speed steel pushed that limit much
higher.

132
00:09:05,804 --> 00:09:10,625
Stellite pushed it higher still, and it
did so in a completely different way.

133
00:09:10,975 --> 00:09:16,455
In steel, hardness comes from a structure
trapped by quenching, and heat lets that

134
00:09:16,455 --> 00:09:17,525
structure relax.

135
00:09:17,875 --> 00:09:23,520
In Stellite, hardness comes from hard
carbides of chromium and tungsten locked

136
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:25,619
inside a tough cobalt matrix.

137
00:09:25,969 --> 00:09:28,379
Heat does not undo that structure.

138
00:09:28,729 --> 00:09:33,994
The alloy is the same at nine hundred
degrees as it was at room temperature,

139
00:09:33,994 --> 00:09:35,449
just a little softer.

140
00:09:36,599 --> 00:09:42,177
Every cutting material that came after
Stellite would follow the same idea: hard

141
00:09:42,177 --> 00:09:45,175
particles held together by a tougher
metal.

142
00:09:45,525 --> 00:09:48,939
Keep that in mind, because it is about to
come back.

143
00:09:50,889 --> 00:09:57,737
In 1912 Haynes formed the Haynes Stellite
Works on Union Street in Kokomo.

144
00:09:58,087 --> 00:10:04,018
In 1915, together with two local
businessmen, he incorporated it as the

145
00:10:04,018 --> 00:10:06,023
Haynes Stellite Company.

146
00:10:07,173 --> 00:10:09,818
The timing could not have been better.

147
00:10:10,168 --> 00:10:16,067
The First World War created a demand for
machined metal on a scale that no one had

148
00:10:16,067 --> 00:10:16,931
planned for.

149
00:10:17,281 --> 00:10:22,914
Shells, gun barrels, engine parts and
aircraft components all had to be turned,

150
00:10:22,914 --> 00:10:25,195
bored and faced, by the million.

151
00:10:25,545 --> 00:10:31,113
Every minute a tool spent cutting instead
of being reground meant more output.

152
00:10:31,463 --> 00:10:37,164
Stellite tools, able to run hot and fast
on tough material, became a serious

153
00:10:37,164 --> 00:10:38,589
industrial product.

154
00:10:39,739 --> 00:10:43,472
The company received lucrative wartime
contracts.

155
00:10:43,822 --> 00:10:48,323
By 1916, Elwood Haynes was a millionaire.

156
00:10:49,473 --> 00:10:55,945
In 1920 Haynes sold the company to Union
Carbide, which was building a large

157
00:10:55,945 --> 00:10:59,351
business in alloys and industrial gases.

158
00:10:59,701 --> 00:11:06,570
Haynes himself died in Kokomo in 1925, at
the age of sixty-seven.

159
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:11,910
He is also remembered as one of the
independent discoverers of stainless

160
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:17,315
steel, an alloy he worked out around the
same time as the English metallurgist

161
00:11:17,315 --> 00:11:18,355
Harry Brearley.

162
00:11:18,705 --> 00:11:24,637
It is a striking record for one man in one
small town: an early car, a family of

163
00:11:24,637 --> 00:11:28,049
cobalt alloys, and a share in stainless
steel.

164
00:11:29,999 --> 00:11:35,070
Under Union Carbide, Stellite found a
second career that would outlast the

165
00:11:35,070 --> 00:11:35,481
first.

166
00:11:36,631 --> 00:11:43,284
In the early 1920s the company developed a
hardfacing process: welding a layer of

167
00:11:43,284 --> 00:11:48,130
Stellite onto the surface of a part made
of ordinary steel.

168
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:53,598
Instead of making the whole part from
expensive cobalt alloy, you could put a

169
00:11:53,598 --> 00:11:56,523
thin, hard skin exactly where wear
happened.

170
00:11:57,673 --> 00:12:01,363
One of the first great uses was in engine
valves.

171
00:12:01,713 --> 00:12:07,274
A poppet valve in an engine slams shut
against its seat thousands of times a

172
00:12:07,274 --> 00:12:10,055
minute, in hot, corrosive exhaust gas.

173
00:12:10,405 --> 00:12:15,077
A Stellite face on the valve and the seat
made them last far longer.

174
00:12:16,227 --> 00:12:21,389
In 1927 Charles Lindbergh flew the Spirit
of St.

175
00:12:21,739 --> 00:12:26,774
Louis from New York to Paris, alone,
nonstop, across the Atlantic.

176
00:12:27,124 --> 00:12:32,244
The engine that carried him included hard
surfaced valves made by Haynes.

177
00:12:32,594 --> 00:12:39,061
The cobalt alloy invented for cutlery in a
small Indiana town was now inside one of

178
00:12:39,061 --> 00:12:41,789
the most famous flights in history.

179
00:12:43,739 --> 00:12:47,493
Back in the machine shop, though, a new
material was coming.

180
00:12:48,643 --> 00:12:54,163
In Germany, researchers at the lamp maker
Osram had been working with tungsten for

181
00:12:54,163 --> 00:12:55,577
light bulb filaments.

182
00:12:55,927 --> 00:13:00,805
Tungsten carbide, a compound of tungsten
and carbon, was known to be

183
00:13:00,805 --> 00:13:05,612
extraordinarily hard, but on its own it
was far too brittle to use.

184
00:13:05,962 --> 00:13:13,636
In 1923 Karl Schröter, head of research at
Osram, filed a patent on a way around the

185
00:13:13,636 --> 00:13:14,367
problem.

186
00:13:14,717 --> 00:13:20,574
He pressed fine tungsten carbide powder
together with a small amount of cobalt and

187
00:13:20,574 --> 00:13:22,717
sintered it into a solid body.

188
00:13:23,067 --> 00:13:28,358
The cobalt melted around the carbide
grains and held them together like cement.

189
00:13:29,508 --> 00:13:35,311
At the end of 1925 Osram sold the license
to Krupp.

190
00:13:35,661 --> 00:13:41,898
In 1926 Krupp brought the material to
market under the name Widia, from the

191
00:13:41,898 --> 00:13:44,642
German wie Diamant, like diamond.

192
00:13:44,992 --> 00:13:53,466
It was shown at the Leipzig fair in 1927,
and by the late 1920s cemented carbide was

193
00:13:53,466 --> 00:13:56,896
reaching shops in America as well.

194
00:13:58,046 --> 00:14:01,971
The early carbide tips were brittle and
tricky to use.

195
00:14:02,321 --> 00:14:07,939
They chipped if the machine vibrated, and
they had to be brazed onto steel shanks by

196
00:14:07,939 --> 00:14:08,273
hand.

197
00:14:08,623 --> 00:14:14,101
But they were far harder than Stellite,
and as machines grew more rigid they could

198
00:14:14,101 --> 00:14:16,773
run at speeds no cast alloy could match.

199
00:14:17,123 --> 00:14:22,875
Reports from the time describe steel being
cut at up to one hundred and fifty meters

200
00:14:22,875 --> 00:14:26,915
per minute, nearly four times faster than
high-speed steel.

201
00:14:28,065 --> 00:14:33,894
Over the following decades, brazed carbide
tips gave way to indexable carbide

202
00:14:33,894 --> 00:14:36,619
inserts, and then to coated inserts.

203
00:14:36,969 --> 00:14:39,423
Carbide took over the cutting edge.

204
00:14:40,573 --> 00:14:42,706
Now notice the irony.

205
00:14:43,056 --> 00:14:49,463
Cemented carbide is made of hard particles
held in a tough metal binder, exactly the

206
00:14:49,463 --> 00:14:51,675
idea that made Stellite work.

207
00:14:52,025 --> 00:14:57,283
And the binder that holds most carbide
together is cobalt, the metal at the heart

208
00:14:57,283 --> 00:14:58,062
of Stellite.

209
00:14:58,412 --> 00:15:03,340
The material that replaced Stellite was
built from its own core ingredient.

210
00:15:05,290 --> 00:15:08,362
Stellite did not lose because it was bad.

211
00:15:08,712 --> 00:15:14,022
It lost because carbide was better at the
one thing that mattered most in metal

212
00:15:14,022 --> 00:15:15,837
cutting: hardness at speed.

213
00:15:16,987 --> 00:15:20,913
A cast cobalt alloy sits in an awkward
middle position.

214
00:15:21,263 --> 00:15:26,575
It is tougher than carbide and better at
high temperature than high-speed steel.

215
00:15:26,925 --> 00:15:30,125
For a while, that middle position was
valuable.

216
00:15:30,475 --> 00:15:36,221
Cast alloy tools were used for heavy cuts
on rough castings and for jobs where

217
00:15:36,221 --> 00:15:37,621
carbide would chip.

218
00:15:37,971 --> 00:15:43,673
But every improvement in carbide, tougher
grades, better geometry, better machines,

219
00:15:43,673 --> 00:15:46,078
squeezed that middle space smaller.

220
00:15:47,228 --> 00:15:49,703
There was also the question of cost.

221
00:15:50,053 --> 00:15:54,618
Cobalt is expensive, and a solid cast tool
uses a lot of it.

222
00:15:54,968 --> 00:16:00,921
A carbide insert uses only a small amount
of cobalt as a binder, and when one edge

223
00:16:00,921 --> 00:16:04,333
wears out, you simply rotate it to a fresh
one.

224
00:16:04,683 --> 00:16:09,675
For a factory counting every cent per
part, the choice became obvious.

225
00:16:10,825 --> 00:16:15,845
By the second half of the twentieth
century, cast cobalt alloys had largely

226
00:16:15,845 --> 00:16:17,652
disappeared from the lathe.

227
00:16:18,002 --> 00:16:21,138
As cutting tools, they had become a
footnote.

228
00:16:23,088 --> 00:16:25,755
And yet Stellite did not disappear.

229
00:16:26,105 --> 00:16:31,665
It moved to places where wear, heat and
corrosion all attack at once, and where

230
00:16:31,665 --> 00:16:34,339
toughness matters as much as hardness.

231
00:16:35,489 --> 00:16:40,615
It is welded onto the teeth of sawmill
blades, where it lets a saw cut through

232
00:16:40,615 --> 00:16:44,492
wood full of grit and sap for far longer
before sharpening.

233
00:16:44,842 --> 00:16:49,834
It still protects valve seats and valves
in engines and in power plants.

234
00:16:50,184 --> 00:16:56,024
It is used on pump impellers, wear rings
and shafts that grind against abrasive

235
00:16:56,024 --> 00:16:56,541
fluids.

236
00:16:56,891 --> 00:17:02,197
The first section of the barrel in some
heavy machine guns is lined with a

237
00:17:02,197 --> 00:17:06,427
Stellite alloy to resist the erosion of
hot propellant gas.

238
00:17:07,577 --> 00:17:10,180
It even found its way into medicine.

239
00:17:10,530 --> 00:17:16,776
The cage of the Starr-Edwards heart valve,
one of the first commercially available

240
00:17:16,776 --> 00:17:22,946
artificial heart valves, first implanted
in 1960, was made from a Stellite alloy.

241
00:17:23,296 --> 00:17:28,181
Cobalt chromium alloys are still used for
dental prostheses today.

242
00:17:29,331 --> 00:17:32,275
The business changed hands many times.

243
00:17:32,625 --> 00:17:38,983
Union Carbide sold the company to the
Cabot Corporation in 1970.

244
00:17:39,333 --> 00:17:45,787
In 1985 Cabot sold the Stellite hardfacing
business, and the rest of the Kokomo

245
00:17:45,787 --> 00:17:52,077
operation became Haynes International, a
maker of high-performance nickel and

246
00:17:52,077 --> 00:17:53,221
cobalt alloys.

247
00:17:53,571 --> 00:18:00,497
The Stellite trademark itself was acquired
by Kennametal in 2012, a company that grew

248
00:18:00,497 --> 00:18:04,408
up making, of all things, carbide cutting
tools.

249
00:18:06,358 --> 00:18:11,371
Stellite lost the race it became famous
for, but it never lost its edge.

250
00:18:12,521 --> 00:18:16,660
Its story is the story of almost every
cutting material.

251
00:18:17,010 --> 00:18:18,247
A breakthrough.

252
00:18:18,597 --> 00:18:20,069
A golden era.

253
00:18:20,419 --> 00:18:21,934
A faster rival.

254
00:18:22,284 --> 00:18:25,847
And then a second life somewhere nobody
expected.

255
00:18:26,197 --> 00:18:29,503
Carbon steel gave way to tungsten steel.

256
00:18:29,853 --> 00:18:33,352
Tungsten steel gave way to high-speed
steel.

257
00:18:33,702 --> 00:18:37,350
High-speed steel and Stellite gave way to
carbide.

258
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:43,710
And carbide itself now shares the edge
with ceramics, cubic boron nitride and

259
00:18:43,710 --> 00:18:44,335
diamond.

260
00:18:45,485 --> 00:18:48,045
What stays the same is the problem.

261
00:18:48,395 --> 00:18:54,069
A small, hot, punishing zone at the tip of
a tool, where metal meets metal and the

262
00:18:54,069 --> 00:18:56,629
material that survives the heat wins.

263
00:18:57,779 --> 00:19:02,665
Elwood Haynes went looking for a metal
that would not tarnish on a dinner table.

264
00:19:03,015 --> 00:19:08,765
He found one that changed how the world
cut steel, flew across the Atlantic inside

265
00:19:08,765 --> 00:19:14,306
a famous engine, and still guards saw
teeth and valve seats more than a century

266
00:19:14,306 --> 00:19:14,727
later.

267
00:19:15,877 --> 00:19:18,309
That is what happened to Stellite.

268
00:19:19,459 --> 00:19:24,732
If you want to see what came after it, why
carbide really works, and how a modern

269
00:19:24,732 --> 00:19:28,248
insert can cut hardened steel, stay with
this channel.

270
00:19:28,598 --> 00:19:33,483
And tell us in the comments which
forgotten tool we should open up next.

